"This distinction matters because it changes what counts as evidence. Looking for a meridian with a scalpel is like looking for a \"trade route\" with a shovel — you won't find a paved road, because a trade route was never a paved road to begin with. It was a pattern of consistent, repeated connection between two places. Meridians ask to be evaluated the same way: not by dissection, but by whether the pattern of connection they describe holds up, case after case.": "289717ffc237",
"When Western readers first hear about meridians — the channels along which qi is said to travel — the natural assumption is that they must be physical tubes, something like blood vessels or nerves, just not yet found by modern dissection. This assumption is understandable, but it leads to a dead end. No meridian has ever been isolated under a microscope as a distinct anatomical structure, and Chinese medicine doesn't actually claim they will be. A meridian is better understood as a map of connection and influence, not a literal pipe carrying a literal substance.": "597d41e2b208",
